# Generated media and disks — rebuild with `irgo-winvm iso-create` / `vm-create`
*.iso
*.dmg
*.img
/out/

# Large downloaded media and generated payloads. fetch-iso will populate .cache/;
# nothing here belongs in git.
/.cache/

# Probe binaries built by `mise run probes`. Rebuildable from source in seconds;
# they exist so a restart does not lose them, not so they are committed.
/.bin/

# go.work files point at absolute paths on one machine. `mise run upstream:verify`
# writes one to test against local glaze/native clones and removes it again.
go.work
go.work.sum

# Release artefacts, built by mise run go:build.
/dist/

# Probe binaries built into their own source directory. Every mise task builds
# with `-o .bin/`, so these only catch a `go build` run by hand — which is how a
# 4.5 MB artefact was committed once, in a repository whose largest real file is
# a screenshot.
#
# Two of these used to name the directory instead of the binary inside it:
#
#   /glaze-probes/verify        <- ignores the whole source directory
#   /glaze-probes/verify/verify <- ignores the binary in it
#
# The first is what was here. Nothing broke, because git keeps tracking a file
# it already tracks — but a new .go file added to either probe was ignored, so
# `git add -A` would have skipped it silently and the push would have been
# missing a file that builds fine on the machine that wrote it. Verified with
# `git check-ignore -v glaze-probes/verify/newfile.go`, which named this rule.
#
# Every name below was measured by running the build, not worked out. The
# obvious rule — "the binary is named after its directory" — is wrong here:
# probe/go.mod says `module nativeprobe`, so building it drops `nativeprobe`,
# and an entry reading /probe/probe silently guards nothing.
/probe/nativeprobe
/glaze-probes/verify/verify
/glaze-probes/verifyevents/verifyevents
/examples/glaze-all/glaze-all

# The generated site. Built by `mise run site:build` from the markdown, which is
# the source of truth; committing the output would be the second copy the
# generator exists to avoid.
/site/dist/

# macOS writes these into any directory Finder has opened. Ignored here rather
# than relying on a global gitignore, because a contributor's machine is not
# this one.
.DS_Store
